I propose a phased implementation: initiate a relational 'reset' focused on capacity and equity, followed by the structural codification of explicit chore definitions. I commit to using asynchronous tools not as a replacement for conversation, but as the low-friction medium to invite the avoiding roommate back into the dialogue. I acknowledge the unresolved problem identified in the objection: if the avoidance is rooted in a broad relational decline rather than chore-related friction, structural tools will remain insufficient. I propose a test for this: if the roommate refuses to engage with the capacity-based framing and the subsequent asynchronous tools, it confirms the problem is interpersonal/emotional rather than organizational.